  "findings": [],
  "limits": [
    "Source approval permits bounded integration and root runtime verification of accepted 3, startup 9, socket 6 and interruption 5 controls; no remedy execution, full capture, Rust gate or merge approval.",
    "Keep root A192 per-listener UDP accounting composition. Only the old Http2Origin class equals the root baseline; the entire canonical parent does not.",
    "One five-second close budget is cooperative. Deadline expiry requests handler cancellation and retains pending tasks/closure slots; it does not establish joined results or forced task termination beyond the budget.",
    "Late callbacks after the observation cutpoint abort their writer without spawning a handler. Their retained closure can require a later observation; completed close does not prove those future callbacks joined.",
    "Completed task cancellations are retained, with no assertion that Python 3.10 distinguishes earlier external cancellation from a requested cleanup cancellation.",
    "Collector batch order is handlers, writer closures, server closure, then acquisition failures; this is observer ordering rather than a global chronological fault sequence.",
    "Interrupted/concurrent close, task-factory failure, repeated callbacks, deadline expiry and simultaneous faults have source assessment only. C-accelerated Future/Task internals were not reviewed.",
    "The existing ordinary handler records partial TLS/H2 errors and preserves its response/EOF policy. Global loop exception-handler restoration and serving-start cancellation remain outside this slice.",
    "The outer capture_run wait_for can translate the cleanup task cancellation into TimeoutError, preserving its cause; no exclusive initiating cancellation identity is claimed across that Task boundary."
  ],
  "assessment": [
    "Synchronous accept registers actual writer-close and canonical handler Tasks before handler polling; failed task creation retains the writer and concrete acquisition errors.",
    "close stops listener acceptance before aborting acquired transports and observes completed results through a concrete registry. Server.wait_closed alone does not prove accepted handlers finished on this CPython version.",
    "asyncio.wait releases its callback/waiter without cancelling the retained children when the caller is interrupted; close retains that primary object and observes within the remaining budget.",
    "Only three concrete peer-disconnect writer-close errors are ignored. Other closure/handler/server errors and child cancellation are retained; no generic catch-all suppression was introduced.",
    "capture_run observes close outcomes and retains cleanup behind its original operation error through the unchanged cycle-aware concrete retention helper."
  ],
  "verification": "Read-only Git/AST/hash comparisons and diff whitespace check; no fixture imports, native commands, Cargo, process probes, source/index changes or push."
